From 10fa425bdb2aed028fc982b71014675d3aa928c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: djvalic <djvalic@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 14:18:30 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] wifi, ad5x: a saved profile put back by the device's state;
 wizard texts fit

Fixes to 0083 and 0084 after review.

0083 updates a saved Wi-Fi profile in place for a join (`nmcli
connection modify`, new psk, psk-flags 0, autoconnect on), then runs
`nmcli connection up`. NetworkManager writes the profile to its file at
once, and Kiln's network hook copies it into wpa_supplicant.conf, so
what the join leaves behind is what the next start joins with. Two ways
that went wrong:

- A join cancelled while `up` ran (another network tapped, the backend
  stopped) returned before the restore: a mistyped password replaced
  the good one, and the printer did not rejoin after a restart.
- run_nmcli() kills nmcli after 30 s, which counted as a failure and
  ran the restore, but NetworkManager's activation goes on. When it
  then succeeded, the profile had its old password back (and
  autoconnect off for a network stock saved disabled): connected now,
  not rejoined after a restart.

nmcli's exit no longer decides. After `up` fails, times out or is
cancelled, the device does (`nmcli -t -f GENERAL.CON-UUID,GENERAL.STATE
device show`, a fixed command line, every device, matched to the
profile's UUID here): the profile up (state 100) keeps it and the join
counts as connected; still activating (40-90) is waited for, once a
second for up to 60 s and only while the join isn't cancelled; anything
else puts the profile back. A cancelled join is put back unless the
profile is already up, and so is one cancelled during the modify
(nmcli may have been killed after NetworkManager took it). The restore
runs uncancellable (run_nmcli(..., false), 10 s limit), so it finishes
before stop() or the next join goes on. It also puts back psk-flags
when they were not 0: NetworkManager then drops the typed psk from the
file as well (checked with NetworkManager 1.58 in a sandbox).

parse_profile_fields() undid terse escapes (`\:`, `\\`) in the values
of `nmcli -s -t -f ... connection show uuid <U>`. nmcli escapes only
tabular terse output; a value on its own line is printed as it is, so
a password with a backslash was read back changed and a restore wrote
the changed one. The value is now the rest of the line after the first
colon (checked with nmcli 1.58: psk `a\:b\\c:d\` and SSID `Cafe:Bar\x`
print unchanged, while `connection show`'s NAME column escapes).

0084: the wizard's toolhead text holds 210 bytes in Russian for a
strand in the tube with the tube warning (buffer 160), and the Z
offset plan for a material without temperatures 233 bytes plus the
material name twice (247 for PETG-CF; buffer 256). The buffers are now
384 and 512 bytes, and copy_if_changed() cuts a text too long for its
subject before a whole UTF-8 character (lv_strlcpy cuts bytes) and
compares the cut text, so it no longer re-copies it every refresh. The
other wizard buffers fit their longest translation (explanations 393
of 512 bytes, action phrases under 100 of 128).

Tests: the parsing case now feeds nmcli's real format (`Cafe:Bar`,
`pass:word` unescaped), plus a password with backslashes and colons
read back byte for byte; the device-state parsing; the restore argv
with psk-flags; the fake nmcli answers `device show` from a file (one
answer, then another) and can run `up` until killed: a join nmcli gave
up on that NetworkManager brings up (kept, connected, no restore), a
join cancelled during `up` by another join (put back before the other
join's connect), and one cancelled once the profile is up (kept).
